Emphysema
Respiratory System
Progressive dyspnea, Pink puffer/cachexia, Hyperinflated lungs/bullae

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-017
Reason for Visit: Acute worsening of severe baseline dyspnea without significant sputum production, causing extreme distress and inability to perform basic self-care.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with known severe emphysema experiencing acute, progressive dyspnea at rest and increased work of breathing. Urgent assessment was required to rule out acute pneumothorax (given the risk of ruptured bullae) and to optimize bronchodilation prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I am fighting for every single breath, even just sitting in this chair, and I feel completely exhausted."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have worsened insidiously over the past 48 hours. The patient reports severe, progressive exertional dyspnea that has now become severe dyspnea at rest. Unlike previous exacerbations, there is minimal to absent cough or sputum production. The patient relies heavily on pursed-lip breathing to maintain airway patency. They report extreme fatigue and inability to sleep due to the constant effort required to breathe.
Functional Status: Highly restricted; currently chair-bound.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of physical exhaustion and inability to call for help if condition worsens.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 28 breaths/min | SpO2: 90% on room air | Wt: 52 Kg
General: Thin, cachectic body habitus ("pink puffer" phenotype), sitting in a tripod position, visibly utilizing accessory muscles of respiration.
Physical Exam: Chest examination reveals a pronounced barrel-shaped chest with an increased anteroposterior diameter. Percussion yields marked hyperresonance bilaterally. Auscultation demonstrates markedly diminished breath sounds globally with a prolonged expiratory phase. No localized wheeze or coarse crackles are present. Previous imaging confirms hyperinflated lungs/bullae.
Environmental Observations: Patient is surrounded by used tissues, but no purulent sputum is visible; oxygen concentrator is running at 1L/min.
Medication Reconciliation: Using LAMA/LABA/ICS inhaler as prescribed; recently finished a course of oral steroids with minimal benefit.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Emphysema - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Spontaneous Pneumothorax - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Diminished breath sounds are bilateral and symmetric, with no acute unilateral hyperresonance or tracheal deviation.

Infective COPD Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of fever, productive cough, or purulent sputum.

Left-Sided Heart Failure - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of bibasilar crackles, elevated JVP, or peripheral edema; "pink puffer" phenotype dominates.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute exacerbation of severe emphysema, characterized by profound dyspnea, tachypnea, and cachexia ("pink puffer" phenotype) without signs of acute bacterial infection. The marked hyperinflation and diminished breath sounds reflect severe loss of elastic recoil. The intense work of breathing (RR 28, accessory muscle use) puts the patient at imminent risk of respiratory muscle fatigue and ventilatory failure. Waiting until the next in-hours period would predictably result in acute decompensation. An urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to rule out a ruptured bulla (pneumothorax), cautiously optimize oxygen therapy without abolishing the hypoxic drive, and deliver high-dose nebulized bronchodilators to reduce dynamic hyperinflation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered continuous nebulized Ipratropium/Salbutamol. Administered oral Prednisolone 40mg to reduce small airway inflammation. Titrated home oxygen via nasal prongs to strictly maintain SpO2 at 88-92%.
Positive Behavioral Change: Place a sturdy chair halfway between the living room and the bathroom; breaking the walking distance into two distinct segments allows your breathing muscles to recover, directly preventing severe drops in oxygen during daily movement.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral for home care assistance with ADLs.
DME/Safety (Optional): Wheeled walker with a seat recommended.
Follow-up: GP review tomorrow to monitor respiratory fatigue; low threshold for hospital admission if accessory muscle use persists.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ambulance transfer (call 000) to the Emergency Department if a sudden, sharp pain occurs on one side of the chest, if confusion develops, or if breathing completely exhausts the patient.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The underlying lung destruction is irreversible; treatments aim only to maximize the function of the remaining lung tissue.
Clinical Course: The extreme breathlessness will ease slightly with the steroids and nebulizers, but the patient remains highly vulnerable to respiratory fatigue.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you feel a sudden pop or sharp pain in your chest, or if you become too exhausted to stay awake.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your lungs have lost their natural elasticity and remain over-inflated; the nebulizers relax the airways to help you push the trapped air out, reducing the work your chest muscles have to do.
Actionable Steps: Actively practice pursed-lip breathing (smell the roses, blow out the candles) during any physical movement; this specific technique creates back-pressure that holds your floppy airways open, allowing trapped air to escape and directly reducing breathlessness.

